 ],
 "literal": "Thinking 'He will not go', I slighted / Thinking 'She will not endure', he slighted / Meanwhile, the conflict made by those two great prides / like a good cobra having bitten, my / distressed heart whirls in pain.",
 "translation": "Thinking 'He will never leave me,' I was proud and careless. Thinking 'She cannot bear my absence,' he too was proud and careless. In the space between these two great prides, a conflict arose. Like one bitten by a deadly cobra, my distressed heart now writhes in agony.",
 "modern": "அவர் என்னை விட்டுப் போகமாட்டார் என்று நான் அலட்சியமாக இருந்தேன். அவள் பிரிவைத் தாங்கமாட்டாள் என்று அவரும் அலட்சியமாக இருந்தார். இந்த இரண்டு பெரிய பெருமைகளுக்கு இடையே ஏற்பட்ட மோதலால், நல்ல பாம்பு கடித்தது போல, என் துன்பப்படும் நெஞ்சம் நிலைதடுமாறித் தவிக்கிறது.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine to her close friend or to herself after the hero has departed on a journey. She reflects on how both she and her lover miscalculated each other's strength and intentions. She assumed his love was too deep for him to ever leave her, so she did not plead with him to stay. He assumed she was too fragile to survive his departure, yet he left anyway, perhaps to test her or out of duty. This clash of 'two great prides' (iru pēr āṇmai) has resulted in her current agony. The pain of separation is compared to the sudden, lethal bite of a cobra (nallarā), whose poison makes her distressed heart whirl in agony.",
